iduals  disease it turned out as I mentioned  that half a percent of our patients have  two or more disorders we developed a  pipeline to also be able to call  structural variation as I mentioned and  20 of our Sol's came from this about 4%  of the solves had large structural  variants six were de novo heterozygous  variants  six were reported as compound heads five  of those it was a large structural  variant together with a small variant  but in one case we actually had the  individual had two structural variants  inherited in the same gene and two cases  we had homozygous inherited variants and  one this was because it was us it was  inherited from a single parent but it  was form as I guessed you to uniparental  I suppose of nought and four of those  cases in the genes shown here that  patient had actually previously been  tested with a gene specific test which  which we then were able to show was not  the case and then we have many other  structural variants there are some of  them are likely to be causal they just  need a little bit more work as an  example this was a adult onset disorder  case a 40 year old female healthy until  32 initially reported with sensory  neural hearing loss been her eyesight  and her memory and decision-making  started to fill she had sensory and  motor neuropathy and then she developed  severe weakness in lure and bulbar  muscles and she was confined most mostly  to a wheelchair or a walker her symptoms  got worse over time they were much more  severely getting worse at the start and  then they they killed off a little bit  she'd seen lots of different specialists  been to many different hospitals this  just shows a variety of the sorts of  interventions that she had already been  through before she came to the UDN lots  of prior genetic workup performed a  whole genome sequencing on her and what  we found is two rare variants in trans  and the SRC 52 a3 riboflavin transporter  which means that she had dry both laven  transporter deficiency and then she also  had a third rare deleterious variant in  ESL core dehydrogenase and those two  different genes that I mentioned are  actually both parts of that same pathway  so this give us a diagnosis and this is  the disorder so it's riboflavin  transporter 2 deficiency one say between  infancy and 30s progressive disorder but  presents with hearing loss often leads  to death due to respiratory failure it's  known that high doses of riboflavin  often really symptoms and one of the  things that was interesting here was  that this individual had actually  received a mitral cocktail which is a  mixture of different combined compounded  drugs because she had had respiratory  failure a few years into her disease and  that has a low dose of riboflavin so  that's probably what made the severity  of her symptoms tail off after a few  years when she was put on this with a  diagnosis that she had they actually  increased the dose of riboflavin by  seven times and one of the great things  that happened was whereas previously she  had no sensation in her feet afterwards  within 48 days she had sensation  returning and increasing improvements  over the next few weeks and months and  before the treatment she took a very  long time to cover a short distance and  that time improved and improved  dramatically there's general agreement  that a response tends to be better and  faster with earlier treatment so this  was an example of a case where doesn't  happen very often but the diagnosis led  to a treatment that helped there are  other types of variation obviously we've  developed methods to identify them this  is just an example of a case from the  Vanderbilt it was a 31 month old male  with a heart defect hypertonia  dysmorphic features and some other  symptoms as shown here able to sit with  their assistance seven months standing  at 24 months but not walking few words  and signs to communicate he did have  hyperpigmentation of the abdomen  eggs that followed the lanes of Blaschka  only family no family history and only  child lots of tests lots of consults and  clinics over two and a half years and no  diagnosis we performed whole genome  sequencing on two skin samples from the  light and dark skin and her standard  methods didn't find anything  but because of that hyper pigmentation  we had started to develop pipeline that  would help us look for camera isms or  mosaicism most people are diploid one  set of chromosomes from mom and one from  dad and when we look at the tests that  we developed a normal individual whose  diploid would have bands at zero fifty  and a hundred but what we saw in this  individual was some extra bands and  19:15 in the later skin sample and 95  and five in the darker what that means  this individual is intermediate between  diploid and trip light so he has some  diploid cells and some triploid he's X Y  X X Y this was confirmed and we now know  that he was a mix applied individual and  we were actually able to identify  another mix employed individual as part  of the UDN so findings to date we have  made diagnosis with small variants  repeat expansions structural variants  including uniparental die semi makes  appointee lytic KC's other types of  chromosomal abnormalities and the  diagnosis are being made in many  different categories so we have known  unrecognized syndromes symptoms that  just weren't previously caught we've  also seen an expansion of phenotypes for  some syndromes so it doesn't quite look  like what it actually was so maybe they  weren't given the right sort of test but  a genome can pick that up in an unbiased  manner but then we've seen novel  phenotypes for a new one gene and new  genes working together with the clinical  colleagues across the whole of the  udn a total of 32 new syndromes were  defined in terms of actionability of  findings within the UDN 84% were  medically actionable by the clinical  teams based on defined agree and agreed  Network criteria many of these 